<% content_for :title, "Publishing — Omarchy Hub" %>
<div class="content-page container">
  <p class="eyebrow">Publishing guide</p>
  <h1>Share your plugins and themes.</h1>
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="statement">Push it anywhere — GitHub, Codeberg, your own Forgejo, or nowhere
      public at all. The registry never requires a forge. Tag, publish, done.</p>

    <h2>1. Scaffold</h2>
    <pre>$ omarchy plugin new you/weather
$ omarchy theme new you/night</pre>
    <p>You get a <code>manifest.json</code>, a starter widget, a readme, and an optional CI
      publish workflow.</p>
    <p>A theme starts with <code>colors.toml</code> and a manifest. Add wallpapers in
      <code>backgrounds/</code> and a <code>preview.png</code>. To start from an installed
      palette, use <code>omarchy theme new you/night --from tokyo-night</code>.</p>

    <h2>2. Claim your namespace</h2>
    <p><%= link_to "Sign in", new_session_path %> (no password — we email you a code), claim
      your handle, and enable two-factor auth. Your plugin's id will be
      <code>handle.name</code>, installed as <code>handle/name</code>.</p>

    <h2>3. Publish</h2>
    <pre>$ omarchy plugin publish
# device flow: approve the 8-char code in your browser, done.
# or, by hand:
$ tar czf weather-1.0.0.tar.gz manifest.json Widget.qml README.md
$ curl -X POST --data-binary @weather-1.0.0.tar.gz \
    -H "Authorization: Bearer omp_…" \
    https://plugins.omarchy.org/api/v1/plugins/you/weather/versions</pre>
    <p>A publish token from <code>omarchy plugin publish</code> is push-only, covers any plugin in
      your namespaces, and expires in 7 days (CI tokens from trusted publishing are minted per
      plugin). Push-only means it can't yank, transfer ownership, or change account settings. Every version —
      first publish or update — runs the same review pipeline: structural validation, malware
      scanning, a capability fingerprint, and a short hold before going live. Versions are
      immutable: once <code>you/weather@1.0.0</code> exists, those bytes are frozen forever.</p>

    <h2>The rules</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Strict semver, always increasing. A used version number is burned forever, even if rejected.</li>
      <li>A declared <code>license</code> must be SPDX. Preserve upstream licenses and attribution when adapting someone else's work. <code>repository</code> is optional; trusted publishing records the exact source commit.</li>
      <li>No symlinks or paths outside the package directory. Plugins allow 10 MB compressed; themes allow 50 MB.</li>
      <li>Manifest <code>id</code> must equal <code>yourhandle.pluginname</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Publishing a theme</h2>
    <pre>$ omarchy theme validate ./omarchy-night-theme
$ omarchy theme publish ./omarchy-night-theme</pre>
    <p>The same account, namespace, review pipeline, hold window, signed downloads and
      revocations cover themes. A theme declares <code>packageType: "theme"</code>,
      <code>kinds: ["theme"]</code> and <code>entryPoints: {"theme": "colors.toml"}</code>.
      A package's type cannot change after its first submission.</p>
    <p>Themes supply palette data, shell appearance, installed icon names and images.
      Omarchy generates application configs. Scripts, Lua, terminal overrides and
      extension installers cannot be published as a theme. Palette values are validated
      before they reach generated configs. Suspicious content still goes through review;
      passing a scan is not a guarantee that every vulnerability has been found.</p>
    <p>The raw upload endpoint is <code>/api/v1/themes/you/night/versions</code>.
      Device login and trusted-publishing tokens work for both package types.</p>

    <h2>Declare Omarchy compatibility</h2>
    <p>Plugins and themes use the same optional compatibility declaration in <code>manifest.json</code>:</p>
    <pre>"compatibility": {
  "apiVersion": 1,
  "minOmarchyVersion": "4.0.4",
  "maxOmarchyVersionExclusive": "5.0.0"
}</pre>
    <p>This example requires Omarchy 4.0.4 or newer, before 5.0.0. Declare only the
      range your package supports. Requirements are shown separately from community
      reports; passing security review does not establish runtime compatibility.</p>
    <p>Users can report working or broken behavior for an exact package release and
      Omarchy version/build. Reports appear as linked community comments, with
      separate private reproduction details. Problem reports notify the publishing
      team. Three independent reports raise a warning; a creator or moderator must
      confirm incompatibility before the companion client blocks installation on the affected build.
      Client enforcement requires that integration; it is not yet part of stock Omarchy.</p>

    <h2>Customize a release</h2>
    <pre>$ omarchy plugin clone you/weather
$ omarchy theme clone you.night my-night --apply</pre>
    <p>Downloads live in your user data directory. Clones live in your config directory
      with their own identity and keep the original installed. They are editable local
      work, outside automatic updates and release scan coverage. No sudo is required.</p>

    <h2>Make it look good</h2>
    <p>Ship one screenshot named <code>preview.png</code> (or <code>.jpg</code>, <code>.webp</code>,
      <code>.gif</code>) at the tarball root and the directory shows it on your card, your plugin
      page gets a click-to-zoom preview, and your share links get a proper card. Animated GIFs stay
      animated on the plugin page. No resizing needed — the registry generates optimized renditions;
      just keep stills under 40 megapixels and recordings at 1440p or below.</p>
    <p>Two optional manifest fields drive browsing:
      <code>category</code> — one of
      <% Registry::Taxonomy::CATEGORIES.each do |c| %><code><%= c %></code><%= " · " unless c == Registry::Taxonomy::CATEGORIES.last %><% end %> —
      and <code>tags</code>, up to <%= Registry::Taxonomy::MAX_TAGS %> from the curated list
      (<% Registry::Taxonomy::TAGS.each do |t| %><code><%= t %></code><%= ", " unless t == Registry::Taxonomy::TAGS.last %><% end %>).
      Missing a tag that obviously should exist? Ask in an issue — the list is curated on purpose,
      so filters stay meaningful.</p>

    <h2>Prefer hands-off?</h2>
    <p>Register <span class="term">trusted publishing</span> from your dashboard (repo +
      workflow + environment, GitHub Actions today) and tagging <code>v1.0.0</code> publishes
      for you via OIDC with no stored secret at all — your plugin page gets provenance links
      back to the exact commit and workflow run.</p>
  </div>
</div>
